-- GENERATED — do not edit.
--
-- A verbatim copy of `supabase/seed/0011_published_equipment_rates.sql`, so the catalog can be applied
-- by `supabase db push` rather than by a direct database connection. The seed
-- file is the source; this is the delivery. `npm run seed:migrations`
-- regenerates it and a governance test fails if the two ever differ.
--
-- Everything below is idempotent — `on conflict do nothing` throughout — so
-- applying it twice changes nothing, which is what makes it safe as a
-- migration.
-- =============================================================================

-- =============================================================================
-- 0010 — A published rate on a machine, and where it came from
--
-- 458 machines with an hourly rate that a person can check.
--
-- The resource catalog arrived with 191 machines and no rates, and the trades
-- pack priced 42 of its own, which left most of the fleet at nothing. A machine
-- with no rate contributes nothing to a line, the line totals, the bid goes out
-- and the machine was on the job. Migration 0128 made that visible; it could not
-- make it go away, because the platform had no rates to give.
--
-- **This is not a rental rate and it is not Caterpillar's.** Cat rental pricing
-- is quoted per dealer and per branch and is not published, so there is nothing
-- to load and inventing it would be the exact failure this schema exists to
-- stop. What is published is FEMA's Schedule of Equipment Rates — the figures
-- the federal government reimburses for applicant-owned equipment, revised
-- annually, used by every state DOT and public agency in the country. Real,
-- citable, and stable enough to stand behind.
--
-- What that rate covers matters more than what it is called: depreciation,
-- overhead, maintenance, field repairs, **fuel**, lubricants, tires and
-- servicing. It is an owning-and-operating cost, which is what an estimating
-- engine wants for a machine a company owns, and it is *not* what a rental desk
-- charges. Anybody bidding a rented machine should price it from their dealer's
-- sheet and let RULE-003 do its job — every other rate source outranks
-- `global_seed`.
--
-- Two consequences of "fuel is already in there", and both are enforced here
-- rather than described:
--
--   * every one of these machines ships with `fuel_gallons_per_hour = 0`, so
--     the engine's fuel bucket does not charge for the same gallon twice. RULE-
--     001 keeps the buckets apart; this keeps them from overlapping.
--   * every one ships with `operator_required = false`. FEMA's schedule is
--     explicitly equipment-only and excludes labor. Marking these as needing an
--     operator would have the engine invent a wage nobody stated, so the crew
--     is added by whoever puts the machine on a line.
--
-- **Seven of the 465 rows are not here.** Three are priced by the mile — a
-- sedan, a police motorcycle and a pickup — and `equipment_rates.hourly_rate`
-- is per hour; loading a per-mile figure into it would be off by however far
-- the truck drove. Four more have a blank unit cell: a log splitter, a widener
-- attachment, a fire tractor ladder and a communications trailer. Their
-- neighbors are hourly and they read as hourly, and reading as hourly is not
-- the same as saying so.
--
-- Two names are requalified with their schedule code — a generator and a sewer
-- inspection camera — because the catalog already has a branded machine under
-- each. Seed 0005 spent forty-nine rows removing exactly that clash, where a
-- search returns three identical-looking things, and adding two back would undo
-- it quietly.
--
-- Classes are assigned by a stated keyword rule against the FEMA family, first
-- match wins, and the 8 rows it cannot place land in `Unclassified
-- Equipment` rather than being pushed into a class that would be wrong. A golf
-- cart filed as earthmoving is worse than a golf cart filed as unclassified,
-- because only one of them is somebody's afternoon to fix.
--
-- Source: FEMA Schedule of Equipment Rates, 2025 edition.
-- https://www.fema.gov/assistance/public/tools-resources/schedule-equipment-rates
-- =============================================================================

insert into library_categories (company_id, kind, name, sort_order)
select null, 'equipment_class', v.name, 880
from (values
  ('Emergency Response Equipment'),
  ('Unclassified Equipment')
) as v(name)
on conflict do nothing;

